# cvitae-agent-runtime holds the API keys, because it is the only process that
# talks to a model. cvitae itself never sees the keys set here, and neither does
# the browser. A user who supplies their own key in cvitae's Settings is the one
# exception: it is forwarded per request and spent for that call only.
# Every value below is optional and shown with its default.

# Which provider drives generation. One of: openrouter, huggingface, openai,
# local. Mirrors cvitae's own AI_PROVIDER so one .env can serve both.
AI_PROVIDER=openrouter

# Overrides the provider's default generation model.
# AI_MODEL=

# Credentials. Only the one matching AI_PROVIDER is needed; "local" needs none.
# A caller may also send its own key with a request — cvitae does when the user
# has entered one in Settings — and that key wins for that call and is kept
# nowhere. These are the fallback, and the only key this process itself owns.
#
# A hosted deployment (RUNTIME_MODE=hosted) should set none of them. Nothing
# there could spend one — a request without its own key is refused before the
# fallback is reached — but a credential in a public environment is one relaxed
# check away from being spent, and the process says so at startup if it finds
# one.
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# HF_TOKEN=
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Where embeddings come from. Kept separate from AI_PROVIDER because the two
# rarely coincide: OpenRouter serves no embedding endpoint at all, so a setup
# generating on OpenRouter still embeds locally through Ollama.
EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=local
EMBEDDING_MODEL=nomic-embed-text

# The local OpenAI-compatible server. Ollama's default; LM Studio uses :1234/v1,
# llama.cpp and vLLM :8080/v1. Must stay on loopback — see providers/resolve.ts.
LOCAL_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1

# Where the runtime keeps its state. The canonical CV document and the derived
# LanceDB index both live under here. Defaults to ~/.cvitae.
# CVITAE_HOME=

# Metadata-only audit log for every model and embedding call. JSONL files are
# written daily under CVITAE_HOME/ai-logs. Prompts, responses, image bytes,
# tool arguments/results and vectors are never written. Set to off to disable.
AI_LOG_MODE=metadata

# Optional log directory override. Relative paths resolve from the process cwd.
# AI_LOG_DIR=

# Port and bind address for the optional HTTP entry point (pnpm dev / serve).
PORT=8788

# Keep this on loopback. The service reads and writes the user's CV data and
# holds the provider credentials; it has no authentication of its own because
# it is not meant to be reachable by anything but this machine.
HOST=127.0.0.1

# Which of the two deployments this is. local (default) | hosted
#
# "local" is everything the rest of this file describes: the keys, the CV, the
# index, and a loopback listener that is safe because of where it listens.
#
# "hosted" is a public deployment that holds no credential and stores nothing.
# Every run must carry its own model.apiKey and is refused without one, so the
# service cannot spend the operator's quota however it is reached. It serves
# extract_cv, translate_cv, analyze_offer and verify_recipient — the four that
# touch no storage — and answers 501 for the rest. netlify.toml sets it, so a
# Netlify deploy is hosted-mode by construction; there is no reason to set it
# by hand except to reproduce that behaviour locally.
#
# See src/server/policy.ts, which is where all of it is enforced.
# RUNTIME_MODE=local

# Optional, and only meaningful when RUNTIME_MODE=hosted. A shared secret the
# caller must present as `Authorization: Bearer <token>`.
#
# Off by default, because hosted mode already spends no money of its own —
# whoever finds the URL gets the compute, not the wallet. This is the lever for
# when the compute itself is worth protecting.
# RUNTIME_TOKEN=

# How long one run may take before it is abandoned, in milliseconds. Generous on
# purpose: it exists to catch a hang, not to enforce a latency budget, and one
# offer was measured at 143s on gemma4:12b against a single local GPU. A caller
# with a tighter deadline sends its own timeoutMs and gets the shorter of the
# two. Leaving it blank uses the default rather than disabling it.
# RUN_TIMEOUT_MS=300000

# Optional. Where cvitae-scrapper is listening.
#
# analyze_offer reads a URL through the scraper first and falls back to a plain
# GET when nothing answers, so leaving this unset costs only the boards a plain
# GET cannot read — which is most of the interesting ones. It also supplies the
# fields the board published as data (salary, company, skills), which are worth
# more than the same values inferred by a small model from the same page.
#
# Set it to match cvitae's own SCRAPER_URL. They are separate processes reading
# the same boards, and a runtime without it is quietly worse at the job than the
# in-process path it replaced.
#
# Defaults to http://127.0.0.1:8787. Set it to empty to skip the attempt.
# SCRAPER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8787

# Optional. Where cvitae-mail is listening.
#
# Drafting and sending job applications from the user's own mailbox. It runs as
# a separate process because of what the credential is: a Gmail token is read
# access to everything the user was ever sent plus the ability to write as them,
# and keeping it out of the process that holds the CV and the provider keys
# means a leak in one is not a leak in both.
#
# Every call through src/mail/client.ts answers `unavailable` when nothing is
# listening, so leaving this unset costs only the feature.
#
# Nothing here is registered as a tool. Drafting is an outbound channel and this
# runtime puts scraped offer text into model context; the two in one tool loop
# is an exfiltration path. See the note at the top of src/mail/client.ts.
#
# Defaults to http://127.0.0.1:8789. Set it to empty to disable it.
# MAIL_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8789

# Optional. A Brave Search API key, for the web tier of verify_recipient.
#
# What it buys: the employer's own website when the board published only their
# name, their careers page, and whichever page carries a contact address. Two
# of the three boards this project reads name a company and no website, so
# without a search the verification has nothing to check the posting against
# and reports that it read one source — the posting itself.
#
# It costs no model call. The engine is asked for URLs, the URLs are fetched,
# and the same regex that reads the posting reads the pages. A search result is
# a pointer: a title or a snippet can move a page up the fetch queue and can
# never name a recipient.
#
# Free tier is 2,000 queries a month at one query per second, which a
# verification spends two or three of. https://brave.com/search/api/
#
# Without a key the tier falls back to a keyless engine that is rate-limited
# without notice and, from some networks, answers with a challenge instead of
# results. That is reported as "the web search did not run" rather than as an
# empty web — but it means the feature is best-effort until a key is set.
# BRAVE_API_KEY=

# Optional. Which engine the web tier uses.
#
# auto (default) picks Brave when BRAVE_API_KEY is set and DuckDuckGo when it is
# not. Naming one explicitly disables the substitution, so a deployment that has
# paid for a key fails loudly instead of quietly scraping a search page.
#
# auto | brave | duckduckgo | off
# WEB_SEARCH=auto

# Optional. Which market to search, as a two-letter country code.
#
# Defaults to pl, because the boards this project reads are Polish and the
# difference is not cosmetic: an unqualified search for a small Polish
# employer's name returns the American company with the same one.
# WEB_SEARCH_COUNTRY=pl

# Optional. How long one search query may take, in milliseconds.
#
# Defaults to 10000. The tier runs two queries in parallel and reads up to three
# pages under a separate 12s ceiling, so this bounds the slowest third of a
# verification a person is waiting on.
# WEB_SEARCH_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
